---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const scCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Computation changed the questions', note: 'Bootstrap resampling, permutation tests and simulation let you answer questions by brute force that no closed-form formula exists for. Fisher imagined shuffling labels in 1935; a laptop does it a million times over lunch.' },
    { math: '2 · The bootstrap', note: 'Resample your own data with replacement thousands of times; the spread of the results is the uncertainty. No normality assumption, no formulas — Efron (1979) built one of statistics\' great ideas out of a repeat loop.' },
    { math: '3 · Simulation as understanding', note: 'Fake data with known truth, run your method, check it recovers the truth. Every serious analysis deserves a simulation study — and Monte Carlo methods (Metropolis, 1953) power modern Bayesian computing.' },
    { math: '4 · Reproducible pipelines', note: 'Scripts (R, Python) that run end-to-end — data in, results out — are the standard of proof. Knitr/Jupyter notebooks, version control and environments make your analysis a re-runnable object, not a memory.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/statistics/expert/statistical-computing">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Statistical computing is where the discipline now lives: <strong>methods that exist only
    because computers do</strong> — resampling, simulation, MCMC, and machine learning itself.
    Just as important is the craft around them: reproducible pipelines that turn an analysis from
    a one-off into a re-runnable scientific object.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the computational turn — four ideas that reshaped statistics" widget="equationStep" config={scCfg} />

  <h2>The working toolkit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>R</strong> — statistics' native tongue: 19,000+ packages, literate reporting via R Markdown, the lingua franca of academic methods (and every new technique lands here first).</li>
    <li><strong>Python</strong> — pandas, numpy, statsmodels, scikit-learn: statistics embedded in a general engineering language, the default in industry data science.</li>
    <li><strong>Probabilistic programming:</strong> Stan and PyMC let you write a Bayesian model in a few lines and let MCMC sample the posterior automatically — custom models without custom derivations.</li>
    <li><strong>Reproducibility crisis meets code:</strong> if the data and code are published, the analysis can be checked; if not, it's a claim. Journals increasingly require both — the pipeline is part of the result.</li>
    <li><strong>Numerical literacy:</strong> floating-point arithmetic, numerical stability (log-sums instead of products of tiny probabilities), and random seeds — the boring details that make results reproducible or irreproducible.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Efron (1979); Metropolis et al. (1953); Wickham & Grolemund, R for Data Science; Efron & Hastie, Computer Age Statistical Inference.</p>
</TopicPage>
